# Auto detect text files and perform LF normalization
* text=auto
*.mp4 filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.bz2 filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text

# Shell scripts must keep LF in the working tree. core.autocrlf is true on the
# machine this repo is maintained from, and a CRLF shebang makes bash fail with
# "bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M".
*.sh text eol=lf

# Generated powder data sheets, their generator and the cut grid. build.mjs
# writes LF and compares byte-for-byte to decide whether a sheet is current, so
# a CRLF working tree makes --check report all sixteen as drifted on every fresh
# clone. Same reason as the shell scripts above.
docs/powder-datasheets/**/*.html text eol=lf
docs/powder-datasheets/*.mjs text eol=lf
docs/powder-datasheets/*.csv text eol=lf

# docs/grades.json has the same problem and went years without the same rule.
# build-grades.mjs writes it with LF and its --check does `cur !== jsonPayload()`,
# a strict string compare, so a CRLF working tree reports it stale even when the
# content is identical. That fired the moment this repo was checked out onto a
# branch - `git status` clean, `--check` claiming drift, and regenerating just
# flipped the file back and forth. The pages do not need a rule because
# build-grades.mjs detects and restores their line endings when it writes them;
# grades.json is the one output it writes unconditionally as LF.
docs/grades.json text eol=lf
